        "type": "textarea",
        "canonical_field": "essay:generated:question_8627781006",
        "action": "fill",
        "value": "I co-founded Open Health Intelligence and built ClarityAtlas, an iOS health product, end to end in Swift and SwiftUI with a Supabase backend. The hardest technical piece was estimating sleep without a wearable. I built a passive sleep-estimation system from phone-interaction and motion signals, pulling HealthKit and Core Motion inputs like step count, gait speed, and movement patterns, and I built backend services for behavioral comparison, including a Sleep Twins matcher using standardized features and weighted distance. We shipped it into a live pilot at Johns Hopkins with 30+ users, and the project was selected for the Pava Spark Accelerator and NSF I-Corps. We also got a non-binding investment indication of roughly $75,000 for about 7 to 8 percent, which we chose not to pursue because we wanted the right long-term partners first. The part I am proudest of is not the code. During Lighting of the Quads, my cofounders and I realized President Daniels was nearby, went to Garland Hall, saw him through locked doors, and knocked until we got to pitch him. That turned into a formal meeting months later. Building the thing was maybe half the work.",
